3 · Learning rate and number of trees trade off: early stopping
Hold out 20 % of each training set as a validation slice, let XGBoost watch the validation log-loss after every tree, and stop when it has not improved for 25 rounds.

fit_with_stop &lt;- function(lr) {
  spec &lt;- boost_tree(trees = 2000, learn_rate = lr, tree_depth = 3, min_n = 20, stop_iter = 25) |&gt;
    set_engine("xgboost", validation = 0.2) |&gt;
    set_mode("classification")
  set.seed(2026)
  workflow(dm_rec, spec) |&gt; fit(dm_train) |&gt; extract_fit_engine()
}

# the evaluation log lives in different places in xgboost 1.x and &gt;= 2.x; this reads both
eval_log &lt;- function(booster) {
  log &lt;- attributes(booster)$evaluation_log
  if (is.null(log)) log &lt;- tryCatch(booster$evaluation_log, error = function(e) NULL)
  as_tibble(log)
}

curves &lt;- purrr::map_dfr(c(0.3, 0.05, 0.01), \(lr) fit_with_stop(lr) |&gt; eval_log() |&gt; mutate(learn_rate = lr))

curves |&gt;
  ggplot(aes(iter, validation_logloss, colour = factor(learn_rate))) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1) +
  labs(x = "number of trees", y = "validation log-loss", colour = "learn_rate",
       title = "Early stopping: where each learning rate stops improving") +
  theme_minimal()







curves |&gt; group_by(learn_rate) |&gt; summarise(best_iter = iter[which.min(validation_logloss)], best_logloss = min(validation_logloss))






A high learning rate reaches its minimum in a few dozen trees and then overfits; a small one takes hundreds of trees to get there and ends lower. Rule: fix a learning rate in 0.01–0.1, then let early stopping or cross-validation choose the number of trees — do not tune both freely.

6 · Class imbalance: the honest version

lr_spec   &lt;- logistic_reg() |&gt; set_engine("glm")
rec_smote &lt;- dm_rec |&gt; step_smote(undx_dm, over_ratio = 1)          # only applied to training data (skip = TRUE)
rec_down  &lt;- dm_rec |&gt; step_downsample(undx_dm, under_ratio = 1)

set.seed(2026)
imbalance_res &lt;- workflow_set(preproc = list(base = dm_rec, smote = rec_smote, downsample = rec_down),
                              models  = list(logistic = lr_spec)) |&gt;
  workflow_map("fit_resamples", resamples = dm_folds, metrics = cls_metrics, seed = 2026)

collect_metrics(imbalance_res) |&gt;
  filter(.metric %in% c("roc_auc", "brier_class", "sensitivity", "specificity")) |&gt;
  select(wflow_id, .metric, mean) |&gt;
  pivot_wider(names_from = .metric, values_from = mean)











Balancing the training set pushes sensitivity-at-0.5 up — and AUC does not move while the Brier score gets worse, because every probability now thinks prevalence is 50 %. You got the same sensitivity in §5 by moving the threshold, without breaking calibration. SMOTE is a last resort; if you use it, recalibrate afterwards.
